1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,318
This is a technical walkthrough of EcoConnectAI as it exists in the repository today.

2
00:00:05,318 --> 00:00:07,070
It is not a marketing video.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:12,012
Every number comes from the project's own output files, configuration and code.

4
00:00:12,012 --> 00:00:16,705
Where something exists only in the paper, or only as a plan, I will say so.

5
00:00:17,605 --> 00:00:18,746
First, one rule.

6
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:23,670
Every result carries one of four labels, and you must never mix them.

7
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:25,025
Published baseline:

8
00:00:25,025 --> 00:00:33,588
the ninety five point five six percent accuracy belongs to the foundation study by Ghorbanian and colleagues, not to us.

9
00:00:33,588 --> 00:00:35,229
Prototype or synthetic:

10
00:00:35,229 --> 00:00:41,651
the paper's Tables six to eight, including patch P16, were computed on synthetic geometry.

11
00:00:41,651 --> 00:00:43,791
Development subset, not final:

12
00:00:43,791 --> 00:00:46,717
real data, a small EfficientNet B0 model.

13
00:00:46,717 --> 00:00:50,142
Everything the app shows today is in this class.

14
00:00:50,142 --> 00:00:51,284
And not yet run:

15
00:00:51,284 --> 00:00:52,711
the full UNB7 model.

16
00:00:53,611 --> 00:00:54,390
The problem.

17
00:00:54,390 --> 00:01:00,297
Mangrove maps tell you where habitat is, but not which patches hold the landscape together.

18
00:01:00,297 --> 00:01:04,516
A small patch can be the only stepping stone between two regions.

19
00:01:04,516 --> 00:01:16,590
EcoConnectAI segments mangrove from Sentinel-1 radar, turns the mask into patches, builds a connectivity graph, and ranks patches by the exact connectivity loss when each one is removed.

20
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:21,719
It then runs what-if scenarios, explains rankings, and ranks restoration sites.

21
00:01:22,619 --> 00:01:25,859
Here is the architecture, mapped to real files.

22
00:01:25,859 --> 00:01:29,168
Sentinel data comes from public STAC catalogues.

23
00:01:29,168 --> 00:01:31,374
Scenes are composited and tiled.

24
00:01:31,374 --> 00:01:35,785
A U-Net with an EfficientNet encoder produces a probability map.

25
00:01:35,785 --> 00:01:41,094
Thresholding and connected components give patches, which become graph nodes.

26
00:01:41,094 --> 00:01:46,677
The graph gives connectivity, criticality, what-if, explanations and restoration.

27
00:01:46,677 --> 00:01:50,882
A Fast A P I backend with SQLite serves a Next J S interface.

28
00:01:51,782 --> 00:01:54,280
The stack, verified from code.

29
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:55,029
Frontend:

30
00:01:55,029 --> 00:02:01,522
Next J S, React, TypeScript, Leaflet maps, React Flow for the graph, Recharts.

31
00:02:01,522 --> 00:02:02,188
Backend:

32
00:02:02,188 --> 00:02:06,933
Fast A P I, SQLAlchemy over SQLite, J W T authentication.

33
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:08,348
Machine learning:

34
00:02:08,348 --> 00:02:12,094
PyTorch with the segmentation models library.

35
00:02:12,094 --> 00:02:13,093
Raster work:

36
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:15,258
rasterio, pyproj, shapely.

37
00:02:15,258 --> 00:02:20,919
The graph mathematics is our own pure Python code, with no NetworkX.

38
00:02:20,919 --> 00:02:23,582
Forty four automated tests pass.

39
00:02:24,482 --> 00:02:26,259
Where the data comes from.

40
00:02:26,259 --> 00:02:33,912
Sentinel-1 radiometrically terrain corrected backscatter, from Microsoft Planetary Computer, is the model input.

41
00:02:33,912 --> 00:02:37,602
Sentinel-2 level 2 A, from Earth Search, is ancillary.

42
00:02:37,602 --> 00:02:41,839
Labels are Global Mangrove Watch version three, twenty twenty.

43
00:02:41,839 --> 00:02:48,262
There is no Kaggle dataset, no Landsat, no field survey, and Google Earth Engine was not used.

44
00:02:49,162 --> 00:02:50,407
Sentinel-1 details.

45
00:02:50,407 --> 00:02:55,191
The product is R T C gamma nought from interferometric wide swath scenes.

46
00:02:55,191 --> 00:02:58,533
We use V V and V H in decibels, with no ratio band.

47
00:02:58,533 --> 00:03:01,351
Terrain correction is done by the provider.

48
00:03:01,351 --> 00:03:08,625
There is no spatial speckle filter; instead a per-pixel temporal median over the year's scenes reduces speckle.

49
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:14,457
Everything is on a ten metre UTM grid, z-score normalised using training statistics only.

50
00:03:14,457 --> 00:03:16,554
The proof it is Sentinel-1 only:

51
00:03:16,554 --> 00:03:22,648
the dataset config selects bands zero and one, and the experiment records two input channels.

52
00:03:22,648 --> 00:03:26,187
Radar is chosen because it sees through monsoon cloud.

53
00:03:27,087 --> 00:03:28,710
Sentinel-2 is ancillary.

54
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:35,745
Six bands plus N D V I and N D W I, cloud masked with the scene classification layer, median composited.

55
00:03:35,745 --> 00:03:40,954
It is used in two ablation experiments, the basemaps, and a restoration rule.

56
00:03:40,954 --> 00:03:43,795
It is not the input of the reported model.

57
00:03:44,695 --> 00:03:45,163
Labels.

58
00:03:45,163 --> 00:03:49,578
We train against Global Mangrove Watch, which is weak supervision.

59
00:03:49,578 --> 00:03:50,581
Say it exactly:

60
00:03:50,581 --> 00:03:58,743
these are reference labels from a published map, not field ground truth, so every accuracy figure is agreement with G M W.

61
00:03:58,743 --> 00:04:00,348
The areas differ hugely:

62
00:04:00,348 --> 00:04:06,302
Kerala has about one hundred hectares of mangrove, Sundarbans nearly fifty nine thousand.

63
00:04:06,302 --> 00:04:11,319
Note the paper mentions Forest Survey of India labels; the code uses G M W.

64
00:04:12,219 --> 00:04:12,954
The dataset.

65
00:04:12,954 --> 00:04:16,509
Scenes are cut into two hundred and fifty six pixel tiles.

66
00:04:16,509 --> 00:04:21,535
One thousand three hundred and seventy three tiles were written across four areas.

67
00:04:21,535 --> 00:04:27,235
The split is by spatial blocks, so overlapping neighbours cannot leak between train and test.

68
00:04:27,235 --> 00:04:34,099
Development mode used eight hundred training, one hundred and ninety five validation and two hundred test tiles.

69
00:04:34,099 --> 00:04:36,612
Augmentation is flips and rotations only.

70
00:04:37,512 --> 00:04:41,170
The model is a U-Net decoder on an EfficientNet encoder.

71
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:47,768
The encoder, pretrained on ImageNet, downsamples the two channel radar tile to learn what is present.

72
00:04:47,768 --> 00:04:55,150
The decoder upsamples back, and skip connections restore where things are, which preserves thin mangrove fringes.

73
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:57,633
A sigmoid gives per-pixel probability.

74
00:04:57,633 --> 00:05:01,095
UNB7 means the B7 encoder, from the foundation study.

75
00:05:01,095 --> 00:05:09,588
What we actually trained is the same U-Net with a B0 encoder, six point two five million parameters, fully fine tuned on a laptop.

76
00:05:10,488 --> 00:05:12,840
This is where the model is built.

77
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:17,617
The alias U N B 7 maps to efficientnet b7, used by the full config.

78
00:05:17,617 --> 00:05:19,827
The development config uses b0.

79
00:05:19,827 --> 00:05:26,528
Every checkpoint stores its encoder name, and all our trained checkpoints say efficientnet b0.

80
00:05:26,528 --> 00:05:30,164
So the code itself stops us calling our model UNB7.

81
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:32,866
Training of the main model.

82
00:05:32,866 --> 00:05:39,471
AdamW, learning rate three times ten to the minus four, cosine schedule, batch eight, forty epochs.

83
00:05:39,471 --> 00:05:44,342
The loss is binary cross entropy plus Dice, which handles rare positives.

84
00:05:44,342 --> 00:05:50,814
The positive weight of twenty five was set for Kerala and makes the four area model over predict:

85
00:05:50,814 --> 00:05:55,752
validation recall zero point nine seven, precision zero point seven eight.

86
00:05:55,752 --> 00:06:00,222
The best checkpoint, by validation I O U, came at epoch thirty two.

87
00:06:00,222 --> 00:06:03,559
Training took about thirty minutes on an Apple M3.

88
00:06:04,459 --> 00:06:06,216
Evaluation, the key table.

89
00:06:06,216 --> 00:06:14,800
The four area model scores test I O U zero point eight four two and F 1 zero point nine one four on two hundred held-out tiles.

90
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,896
That is our development result.

91
00:06:16,896 --> 00:06:23,452
The Kerala-only models are weak, with I O U between zero point zero two and zero point zero five.

92
00:06:23,452 --> 00:06:29,198
The ninety five point five six percent is shown separately as the published baseline.

93
00:06:29,198 --> 00:06:31,090
Our UNB7 run is not yet run.

94
00:06:31,090 --> 00:06:33,591
All metrics are agreement with G M W.

95
00:06:34,491 --> 00:06:36,003
A caveat you must know.

96
00:06:36,003 --> 00:06:42,049
The zero point eight four is pooled over pixels, and most mangrove pixels are in Sundarbans.

97
00:06:42,049 --> 00:06:50,395
By area, Sundarbans has correct mangrove in seventy six of eighty six test tiles, but Kerala has it in only one of forty three.

98
00:06:50,395 --> 00:06:53,681
So for thin fringes the model is not reliable yet.

99
00:06:53,681 --> 00:06:55,258
If asked for your I O U:

100
00:06:55,258 --> 00:07:03,868
zero point eight four on our pooled development split against G M W, driven by Sundarbans, and not the paper's ninety five percent.

101
00:07:04,768 --> 00:07:07,597
These are the real curves and confusion matrix.

102
00:07:07,597 --> 00:07:14,459
Training loss falls from one point eight to zero point three four, and validation I O U peaks at epoch thirty two.

103
00:07:14,459 --> 00:07:19,215
The confusion matrix shows few missed mangrove pixels but more false positives:

104
00:07:19,215 --> 00:07:20,659
the model over predicts.

105
00:07:21,559 --> 00:07:27,194
In the app, the Data and Models page shows these metrics, read from the evaluation files.

106
00:07:27,194 --> 00:07:27,954
One warning:

107
00:07:27,954 --> 00:07:30,866
the green validated model badge is hard-coded.

108
00:07:30,866 --> 00:07:32,638
Call it a development model.

109
00:07:33,538 --> 00:07:35,088
From probability to mask.

110
00:07:35,088 --> 00:07:41,162
Inference uses an overlapping sliding window with smooth blending to produce a probability raster.

111
00:07:41,162 --> 00:07:42,897
A threshold makes it binary.

112
00:07:42,897 --> 00:07:45,500
We calibrated the threshold on test tiles:

113
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:54,302
F 1 rises from zero point nine one two at zero point three to zero point nine three three at zero point seven, so zero point seven was chosen.

114
00:07:54,302 --> 00:07:56,719
But it is the edge of the tested range.

115
00:07:56,719 --> 00:08:01,615
The threshold matters because it changes patch shapes, and therefore the graph.

116
00:08:02,515 --> 00:08:03,657
Patch extraction.

117
00:08:03,657 --> 00:08:10,174
The mask is split into eight-connected components, and components under two hectares are dropped.

118
00:08:10,174 --> 00:08:17,027
Each patch stores an id, area, centroid, confidence, which is the mean probability, class and polygon.

119
00:08:17,027 --> 00:08:21,662
In the current Kerala run, sixty components gave twenty four patches.

120
00:08:21,662 --> 00:08:22,267
Remember:

121
00:08:22,267 --> 00:08:23,678
size is an attribute.

122
00:08:23,678 --> 00:08:26,768
Importance comes from position in the network.

123
00:08:27,668 --> 00:08:28,305
The graph.

124
00:08:28,305 --> 00:08:29,641
Each patch is a node.

125
00:08:29,641 --> 00:08:37,912
For every patch we take its three nearest patches by centroid distance, and keep a link only if it is within tau, five kilometres.

126
00:08:37,912 --> 00:08:41,793
Each edge is weighted by confidence and decays with distance.

127
00:08:41,793 --> 00:08:44,147
Tau stands in for dispersal distance.

128
00:08:44,147 --> 00:08:51,782
It is an assumption, not calibrated for a species, so every run also repeats the analysis at three and eight kilometres.

129
00:08:52,682 --> 00:08:53,251
In code:

130
00:08:53,251 --> 00:08:58,942
sort by distance, take the first k, keep those within tau, store each pair once.

131
00:08:58,942 --> 00:09:02,357
The weight is exactly equation six of the paper.

132
00:09:03,257 --> 00:09:03,960
The metric.

133
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:07,286
We want one number for how connected the network is.

134
00:09:07,286 --> 00:09:10,996
The headline is I I C, the integral index of connectivity.

135
00:09:10,996 --> 00:09:19,632
For every pair of patches, multiply their areas and divide by one plus the number of links between them; unreachable pairs add nothing.

136
00:09:19,632 --> 00:09:23,853
So I I C rewards large patches that reach each other in few steps.

137
00:09:23,853 --> 00:09:25,708
We also report P C and E C A.

138
00:09:25,708 --> 00:09:33,768
The paper's equation seven score is only an interface score; it can even rise when a patch is removed, so decisions use I I C.

139
00:09:34,668 --> 00:09:37,064
Criticality, the core contribution.

140
00:09:37,064 --> 00:09:39,118
Compute baseline connectivity.

141
00:09:39,118 --> 00:09:41,240
Remove one patch and its edges.

142
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:41,925
Recompute.

143
00:09:41,925 --> 00:09:44,526
The loss divided by the baseline is S.

144
00:09:44,526 --> 00:09:46,717
Repeat for every patch and rank.

145
00:09:46,717 --> 00:09:47,675
This is exact:

146
00:09:47,675 --> 00:09:51,852
the graph is really rebuilt each time, with no approximation.

147
00:09:51,852 --> 00:09:56,849
We also record degree, neighbours, and whether the patch is a cut vertex.

148
00:09:57,749 --> 00:09:58,509
Graph terms.

149
00:09:58,509 --> 00:10:05,411
A node is a patch, an edge a link, degree the number of links, a component a group that can reach each other.

150
00:10:05,411 --> 00:10:12,440
A cut vertex is a node whose removal increases the number of components; the interface calls it a bridge patch.

151
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:15,352
Strictly, a bridge in graph theory is an edge.

152
00:10:15,352 --> 00:10:18,961
In the picture, removing small patch B separates A and C.

153
00:10:18,961 --> 00:10:22,887
Ecologically, they can no longer exchange propagules or fauna.

154
00:10:23,787 --> 00:10:26,988
A real example from the current Kerala run.

155
00:10:26,988 --> 00:10:35,695
The largest patch, P01, thirty five hectares, ranks first with a thirty one percent loss, but it is not a cut vertex.

156
00:10:35,695 --> 00:10:36,290
Now P17:

157
00:10:36,290 --> 00:10:49,836
only three point one hectares, seventeenth by size, yet removing it cuts I I C by twenty seven percent, ranking third, because it is a cut vertex splitting two components into three.

158
00:10:49,836 --> 00:10:56,459
In Odisha, patch P09, fifty five hectares, outranks P04 at nine hundred and ten hectares.

159
00:10:56,459 --> 00:10:57,427
And remember:

160
00:10:57,427 --> 00:11:00,478
P16 exists only in the synthetic results.

161
00:11:01,378 --> 00:11:05,382
Here is P17 in the app, with the evidence drawer open:

162
00:11:05,382 --> 00:11:11,831
the decision, then C of G, C of G minus v, delta C and S, then geometry and neighbours.

163
00:11:11,831 --> 00:11:15,983
Everything is read from stored run files, not generated.

164
00:11:16,883 --> 00:11:19,670
The graph view, drawn with React Flow.

165
00:11:19,670 --> 00:11:20,770
One inaccuracy:

166
00:11:20,770 --> 00:11:29,424
the critical bridges box lists every patch with S above zero point two five, including P01, which is not a cut vertex.

167
00:11:29,424 --> 00:11:30,964
Only P07 and P17 are.

168
00:11:30,964 --> 00:11:33,091
Correct it yourself if asked.

169
00:11:33,991 --> 00:11:35,276
The what-if engine:

170
00:11:35,276 --> 00:11:38,997
baseline, action, exact rebuild, recompute, difference.

171
00:11:38,997 --> 00:11:44,882
Removing P17 costs twenty seven percent of I I C for one point four percent of habitat.

172
00:11:44,882 --> 00:11:49,618
Removing P01 costs thirty one percent, but sixteen percent of habitat.

173
00:11:49,618 --> 00:11:52,459
Restoring C1 adds one point three percent.

174
00:11:52,459 --> 00:11:57,127
Changing tau from three to eight kilometres keeps the ranking stable.

175
00:11:57,127 --> 00:12:01,591
Raising the threshold drops patches from twenty six to twenty one.

176
00:12:01,591 --> 00:12:03,147
This is not a forecast.

177
00:12:03,147 --> 00:12:07,950
It is a what-if under stated assumptions, and it is labelled simulated.

178
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:11,937
On the dashboard I selected P17 and pressed remove.

179
00:12:11,937 --> 00:12:18,171
The server rebuilt the graph and returned minus twenty seven percent I I C and two to three components.

180
00:12:18,171 --> 00:12:22,832
The paper's prototype did this heuristically in the browser; now it is exact.

181
00:12:23,732 --> 00:12:25,834
The Scenario Lab has seven types.

182
00:12:25,834 --> 00:12:33,858
Here, tau sensitivity and threshold sensitivity, both labelled simulated, each with an explanation built from computed values.

183
00:12:33,858 --> 00:12:39,208
These are your evidence-based answers to why five kilometres and why this threshold.

184
00:12:40,108 --> 00:12:41,243
Explainability.

185
00:12:41,243 --> 00:12:44,192
The system does not just print a score.

186
00:12:44,192 --> 00:12:46,537
Here is P17's real explanation:

187
00:12:46,537 --> 00:12:52,059
rank, loss, area, links with distances, cut vertex status and confidence.

188
00:12:52,059 --> 00:12:55,840
Each sentence appears only if its evidence exists.

189
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,488
There is no SHAP, LIME or Grad-CAM.

190
00:12:58,488 --> 00:13:02,042
This is graph-level, rule-based explainability.

191
00:13:02,942 --> 00:13:09,712
The assistant answers by matching an intent and filling templates from stored files, with sources and the result label.

192
00:13:09,712 --> 00:13:13,296
It is not a large language model, and it never invents figures.

193
00:13:14,196 --> 00:13:16,571
Restoration asks the reverse question:

194
00:13:16,571 --> 00:13:18,008
what if we add a patch?

195
00:13:18,008 --> 00:13:26,132
Each candidate is inserted as a node linked to its nearest patches, connectivity is recomputed, and candidates are ranked by gain.

196
00:13:26,132 --> 00:13:30,569
Candidates are areas of marginal model probability, not surveyed sites.

197
00:13:30,569 --> 00:13:32,319
There are no invented costs:

198
00:13:32,319 --> 00:13:37,818
candidates are ranked by connectivity gain because validated cost data is not available.

199
00:13:37,818 --> 00:13:41,192
Uploading a real cost table switches to gain per cost.

200
00:13:42,092 --> 00:13:43,666
The planner for Kerala:

201
00:13:43,666 --> 00:13:47,704
C1 adds one point three percent I I C with three new links.

202
00:13:47,704 --> 00:13:54,890
The panel separates why recommended, why not, and not assessed, such as legal status, ownership and cost.

203
00:13:54,890 --> 00:13:58,312
Recommended is a rule output, not a field verdict.

204
00:13:59,212 --> 00:14:01,690
Change detection is partly implemented.

205
00:14:01,690 --> 00:14:05,758
Kerala has a second Sentinel-1 composite for twenty twenty five.

206
00:14:05,758 --> 00:14:13,068
Running the same model on both years gives three hundred and eighty two versus three hundred and nineteen hectares.

207
00:14:13,068 --> 00:14:15,483
It is labelled observed, model output.

208
00:14:15,483 --> 00:14:21,458
But this model's Kerala I O U is zero point zero two, so this may be noise, not measured loss.

209
00:14:21,458 --> 00:14:27,751
Also, the dashboard change chart currently compares runs of different models, so ignore its deltas.

210
00:14:28,651 --> 00:14:30,067
The four study areas.

211
00:14:30,067 --> 00:14:35,734
Kerala, a Ramsar backwater with thin fringes, is the hardest and most complete case.

212
00:14:35,734 --> 00:14:40,997
Sundarbans is the largest mangrove system, with fifty four patches in our run.

213
00:14:40,997 --> 00:14:45,247
Gulf of Mannar has very sparse mangrove, and Odisha is a delta.

214
00:14:45,247 --> 00:14:50,307
Predicted habitat exceeds G M W everywhere, which confirms over prediction.

215
00:14:50,307 --> 00:14:57,255
And note the paper lists Landsat 9 for Gulf of Mannar; the implementation uses Sentinel-1 for all four.

216
00:14:58,155 --> 00:14:59,279
A short U I tour.

217
00:14:59,279 --> 00:15:07,805
The dashboard shows patches, candidates, critical patches and links on real imagery, with basemaps from our own Sentinel rasters.

218
00:15:07,805 --> 00:15:10,118
The cards come from the run bundle.

219
00:15:10,118 --> 00:15:14,018
The map strip says real data, development model, not final.

220
00:15:14,918 --> 00:15:24,747
The interactive map overlays the raw model probability raster under the patches, and the sensitivity explorer re-runs the analysis with another tau, k or metric.

221
00:15:24,747 --> 00:15:29,753
The small importance timeline in the inspector is synthetic, so do not present it.

222
00:15:30,653 --> 00:15:31,843
Workflow screens:

223
00:15:31,843 --> 00:15:41,292
rule-based alerts, field tasks with photo evidence and verification, JSON reports, and a New Analysis page that runs the full pipeline.

224
00:15:41,292 --> 00:15:43,182
These work with demo users.

225
00:15:43,182 --> 00:15:48,221
There is no real field data, and no deployment with a forest department.

226
00:15:49,121 --> 00:15:50,618
The backend is real.

227
00:15:50,618 --> 00:15:53,761
Fast A P I with SQLite and fifteen tables:

228
00:15:53,761 --> 00:16:05,585
users and roles, a registry of scenes and models, analysis versions, detections, alerts, field tasks, evidence, projects, scenarios, reports and an audit log.

229
00:16:05,585 --> 00:16:07,157
Rasters stay on disk.

230
00:16:07,157 --> 00:16:07,830
The gaps:

231
00:16:07,830 --> 00:16:15,987
many analysis endpoints need no login, there are no migrations, and the paper still says there is no backend.

232
00:16:16,887 --> 00:16:19,485
This slide maps every stage to its folder.

233
00:16:19,485 --> 00:16:24,433
Data acquisition lives in the gee folder and the acquisition and tiling scripts.

234
00:16:24,433 --> 00:16:27,526
The machine learning lives in ecoconnect slash ml.

235
00:16:27,526 --> 00:16:31,917
The graph analysis lives in ecoconnect slash graph, one file per stage.

236
00:16:31,917 --> 00:16:38,288
The backend is in the backend folder, the interface in frontend slash app, and every result in outputs.

237
00:16:38,288 --> 00:16:39,896
Pause here if you need to.

238
00:16:40,796 --> 00:16:41,995
Paper alignment.

239
00:16:41,995 --> 00:16:42,670
Matching:

240
00:16:42,670 --> 00:16:51,814
the equations for patches, edges, I I C, criticality, what-if and restoration, and the synthetic tables reproduce exactly.

241
00:16:51,814 --> 00:16:52,938
Paper outdated:

242
00:16:52,938 --> 00:17:04,031
it says no model, no backend and synthetic data, but now there are trained models, a backend and real runs; labels, sensors and classes also differ.

243
00:17:04,031 --> 00:17:05,231
Not implemented:

244
00:17:05,231 --> 00:17:10,552
UNB7 training, field validation, species-calibrated tau and real costs.

245
00:17:11,452 --> 00:17:14,476
Limitations, which you should state first.

246
00:17:14,476 --> 00:17:16,132
A development B0 model.

247
00:17:16,132 --> 00:17:16,996
Weak labels.

248
00:17:16,996 --> 00:17:19,156
Accuracy driven by Sundarbans.

249
00:17:19,156 --> 00:17:20,308
Over prediction.

250
00:17:20,308 --> 00:17:22,972
A threshold at the edge of the sweep.

251
00:17:22,972 --> 00:17:24,772
Tau and k as assumptions.

252
00:17:24,772 --> 00:17:26,428
Noisy change detection.

253
00:17:26,428 --> 00:17:29,452
Restoration without costs or field checks.

254
00:17:29,452 --> 00:17:31,900
No field validation or deployment.

255
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:34,050
In simple words.

256
00:17:34,050 --> 00:17:34,675
Observe:

257
00:17:34,675 --> 00:17:35,925
satellite radar.

258
00:17:35,925 --> 00:17:36,784
Understand:

259
00:17:36,784 --> 00:17:40,299
a U-Net gives mangrove probability per pixel.

260
00:17:40,299 --> 00:17:40,924
Connect:

261
00:17:40,924 --> 00:17:44,595
patches become nodes, nearby patches get edges.

262
00:17:44,595 --> 00:17:45,298
Identify:

263
00:17:45,298 --> 00:17:48,970
delete each node and measure connectivity loss.

264
00:17:48,970 --> 00:17:49,673
Simulate:

265
00:17:49,673 --> 00:17:52,172
try interventions and recompute.

266
00:17:52,172 --> 00:17:53,032
Prioritise:

267
00:17:53,032 --> 00:17:55,453
rank restoration sites by gain.

268
00:17:55,453 --> 00:17:55,766
Act:

269
00:17:55,766 --> 00:18:00,296
evidence, alerts and field tasks, so that officers decide.

270
00:18:01,196 --> 00:18:03,165
Things you must not claim.

271
00:18:03,165 --> 00:18:05,664
Not ninety five percent accuracy.

272
00:18:05,664 --> 00:18:06,346
Not UNB7.

273
00:18:06,346 --> 00:18:08,163
Not ground truth labels.

274
00:18:08,163 --> 00:18:09,375
Not P16 as real.

275
00:18:09,375 --> 00:18:11,041
Not Landsat or Kaggle.

276
00:18:11,041 --> 00:18:12,252
Not rupee costs.

277
00:18:12,252 --> 00:18:15,887
Not a measured sixteen percent mangrove decline.

278
00:18:15,887 --> 00:18:20,431
Not real-time monitoring, deployment, or field verification.

279
00:18:20,431 --> 00:18:22,021
Not an L L M or SHAP.

280
00:18:22,021 --> 00:18:27,474
And not the landing page numbers, which are hard-coded synthetic values.

281
00:18:28,374 --> 00:18:29,934
The sixty second answer.

282
00:18:29,934 --> 00:18:34,808
EcoConnectAI is a satellite-driven conservation decision support framework.

283
00:18:34,808 --> 00:18:50,992
It maps mangrove from Sentinel-1 radar with a U-Net trained on Global Mangrove Watch weak labels, converts the map into patches and a connectivity graph, and finds the patches whose removal most reduces connectivity, which are often not the largest.

284
00:18:50,992 --> 00:18:57,102
It runs what-if scenarios, explains every ranking, and ranks restoration by connectivity gain.

285
00:18:57,102 --> 00:19:05,031
Today it runs on real data for four Indian landscapes with a development model; UNB7, field validation and costs are next.

286
00:19:05,031 --> 00:19:06,266
Good luck tomorrow.
